@@ -99,7 +99,7 @@ The engine (`fleet/run-engine.mjs`) returns a single structured report object; `
 | `tasks[].examEdited` | no | Present on every row returned after the implementer when the wave-0 examiner recorded an exam (#553): the `proofTests` paths whose blob no longer matches what the examiner left — edited by a fix round (since #653 the implementer never holds the exam while it works, so only a fix round can move it) — `[]` when none moved. The driver never refuses the edit: it names the paths to the reviewer as `EXAM EDITED` and pushes one matching `judgmentCalls` entry; the reviewer decides whether the exam was wrong (reviewer.md rule 8). Absent when no exam was recorded. |
 | `tasks[].baseCorrected` | no | The #314 provisioning-drift record. `null` when the implementer's reported `startHead` (its pre-work `git rev-parse HEAD`, a required implementer-schema field) equalled the dispatched `BASE`; `{ from, to }` when it differed and the implementer reset to `BASE` (`from` = the sha the worktree was provisioned at, `to` = `BASE`); absent when no implementer reply was received (`agent-error`, infra-park). Engine-derived, never model-typed; the predicate is exact equality, not ancestry. Every correction also pushes an `autonomy` judgment call naming both shas; a reply with no `startHead` (engine-bypass class — the schema requires it) pushes a `degradation` call `BASE anchoring unverified` instead. Fix-round replies are not compared: their `BASE` is the prior implementation HEAD by design. |
 | `tests` | yes | Result of the suite run on the integration branch: `{ command, passed, output, unattributed }`. `unattributed` is the run's **unattributed reds** (#871) — every test path a wave adopted red because no task of that wave named it, in wave order and once each; `[]` when there was none, on a green run, and on a run that merged nothing (the key is always present). A path is attributed when it equals an entry of some wave task's `files` or `proofTests` — string equality on the path the plan spelled, no globbing and no resolving — and a candidate takes the unattributed route only when its failing paths are non-empty and every one of them is unattributed: one attributed path, or an output that named no path at all, sends the candidate down the `reconcile:` route and on to `TEST_FAILED` as before. An unattributed candidate is adopted exactly as a green one is (same `MERGED` `waveMerges` row, whose `suite` reads `{ passed: false, unattributed, output }`), no `reconcile:` agent is dispatched at it, and each path draws one `judgmentCalls` line `unattributed red: <path> went red on wave <n>'s fold; no task names it`. The gate reads this field: a `passed: false` with a non-empty `unattributed` passes rather than blocks, and the gate receipt's `suite` carries the list |
-| `integratedRuns` | no | The driver's own execution of every merged task's Proof `Run:` commands, re-run on the adopted integration tree — the evidence the completeness critic reads as INTEGRATED RUN EVIDENCE. One item per command, `{ task, cmd, exit, stdout }`: the task id, the command verbatim, its exit code, and its combined stdout/stderr tail. `[]` when no merged task declared a `Run:` proof and when no tree was adopted — the two cases are indistinguishable here, so read it beside `waveMerges`. A non-zero `exit` is a blocking completeness finding: a task's Proof passed against its own clone but does not hold on the integrated tree, which is precisely what the per-task reviews cannot see. Because the driver ran these itself, they are settled — a cannot-verify item asking for their re-execution is answered by this field, not deferred |
+| `integratedRuns` | no | The driver's own execution of the Proof `Run:` commands of every merged task the fold JOINED (#887), re-run on the adopted integration tree — the evidence the completeness critic reads as INTEGRATED RUN EVIDENCE. A task is joined when its touch set (declared `files` ∪ the paths its captured patch changed) meets another merged task's, which is the only case where the fold can break a proof its own clone already passed; a single-task wave and a wave of pairwise-disjoint tasks re-run nothing. One item per executed command, `{ task, cmd, exit, stdout, joined, with }`: the task id, the command verbatim, its exit code, its combined stdout/stderr tail, the joined paths this task shares (`waveMerges[w].joined` ∩ its touch set) and the ids of the tasks it shares them with, in plan order. `[]` when no joined task declared a `Run:` proof, when the wave joined nothing and when no tree was adopted — the cases are indistinguishable here, so read it beside `waveMerges` and its `joined`. A non-zero `exit` is **reported, not gated** (#871 decision 1): the run carries one `judgmentCalls` line naming the command, the joined path and the pair it went red on, and nothing blocks — a red on the fold of two tasks is a finding about the join, not a finding against either task, so it is not a blocking one. Because the driver ran these itself, they are settled — a cannot-verify item asking for their re-execution is answered by this field, not deferred |
 | `integratedChecks` | no | The driver's own execution of every Global Constraints `Check:` command on the adopted integration tree — the evidence the completeness critic reads as INTEGRATED CHECK EVIDENCE. One item per command, `{ cmd, exit, stdout, minor }`: the command verbatim, its exit code, its combined stdout/stderr tail, and whether the run declared it minor. `[]` when the run declared no checks and when no tree was adopted — the two cases are indistinguishable here, so read it beside `waveMerges`. A non-zero `exit` on a non-minor check is a blocking completeness finding (`integrated Check: <cmd> exited <n> on the adopted tree`): the constraint held in every task's own clone, where each reviewer was right about the tree it read, and the fold broke it — which is precisely what no per-task review can see. A non-zero minor check is a judgment call and nothing more |
 | `reviewEconomy` | no | What a reviewer-minute bought. `reviewerMs` — the summed wall clock of every `review:` agent call, each timed INDIVIDUALLY, so a concurrent pair contributes both durations (both were paid for; a per-round maximum would report a pair as costing what one reviewer cost). `blockingFindings` — how many blocking issues the REVIEWERS returned, de-duplicated by `severity|detail` across the run; the driver's own `Run:`/`Check:` reds are excluded, because charging the driver's findings to the reviewers flatters the ratio. `blockingPerReviewerMinute` — `blockingFindings / (reviewerMs / 60000)`, and `0` when no reviewer ran. `pairRounds` — how many review rounds dispatched a pair. `r2MarginalBlocking` — across those rounds, how many blocking issues the second reviewer returned that the first did not: the number that says whether the pair profile earned its second bill. `refereeFindings` — every finding the driver's mechanical referee returned across the run (all severities, all rounds). `refereeBlocking` — how many of those were blocking. `refereeSkippedPairs` — how many rounds a `peer` task dispatched one reviewer rather than a pair because a blocking referee finding had just been repaired |
 | `baseline` | no | Result of the suite run on BASE's tree in a clone of its own, `<runDir>/clones/baseline` — started during **Setup**, concurrently with wave 1 and never ahead of its dispatch, and run **exactly once per run** whatever the waves do. `{ passed, output }` on every run that completed Setup — never `null` there; `null` only on a run that never got that far. `passed: false` means BASE itself was red, and the run PARKS on it: that wave is `TEST_FAILED` with a `detail` beginning `baseline: the suite is RED on BASE`, every later wave is `SKIPPED`, no `reconcile:` agent is ever dispatched (there is no red for one to repair that the run did not inherit), and the integration branch is left where the wave found it. A red baseline also draws a `baseline:` judgment call. A green one is what makes every later candidate red attributable to the wave |
